---
import { sitePath } from '../data/site';
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { source, release } from '../data/site';
const rows = [
 ['HTTP/1', 'Fresh requests and checked responses', 'Dynamic response bindings need an explicit scenario; this is not a browser or an HTTP/2/3 replay engine.'],
 ['DNS over TCP', 'Framed queries and response comparison', 'DNS over UDP uses a packet/datagram route and has different interface requirements.'],
 ['Modbus TCP / TLS', 'Transaction-aware application replay on fresh connections', 'TLS requires recoverable Modbus messages. Client-certificate authentication and full Modbus Security are not supported. Use a dedicated test device for state-changing operations.'],
 ['DNP3', 'Supported application exchanges and fragment confirmations', 'Unsupported object layouts and Secure Authentication stop with an explanation.'],
 ['MQTT 3.1.1 / 5', 'Fresh connect, identifier handling, maintenance', 'Keepalive handling is supported; enhanced authentication is not.'],
 ['FTP / FTPS', 'Coordinated control and data transfers', 'Secure control/data recovery requires matching keys and complete transfer evidence.'],
 ['TLS', 'Fresh verified handshake; application replay when secrets are available', 'Without secrets, application replay stays incomplete. Embedded TLSK or an explicit matching key log enables supported inner protocols.'],
 ['SSH', 'Explicit commands on a fresh authenticated session', 'Requires credentials and a pinned host key; ciphertext does not reveal commands.'],
 ['Generic TCP', 'Advanced stateful packet engine or explicit packet replay', 'No automatic application semantics. The raw engine is not a complete OS TCP stack.'],
 ['UDP / ICMP', 'Datagram / echo routes or stateless replay', 'Packet privileges and an appropriate interface are required for raw routes.'],
];
---
<Guide title="Protocol support" description="Choose a supported application workflow, or deliberately work at the packet level. Each path has a different evidence boundary." sections={[{id:'matrix',label:'Support matrix'},{id:'transport',label:'Transport boundaries'},{id:'qualification',label:'What was qualified'}]}>
  <h2 id="matrix">Application and packet workflows</h2><p>Support is scoped to the exchange and its captured evidence. Detection alone does not guarantee that a recording contains enough material to recreate a complete application session.</p><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Protocol support matrix"><table><thead><tr><th>Protocol</th><th>Replay path</th><th>Important boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows.map(row => <tr>{row.map(cell => <td>{cell}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
  <h2 id="transport">TCP state belongs to the live connection</h2><p>Application replay uses the OS TCP stack to manage live sequence numbers, acknowledgments, retransmission, and receive windows. Supported adapters manage application identifiers and maintenance traffic above that connection.</p><p>The advanced packet engine tracks live acknowledgment state, bounded retransmissions, receive ordering, and flow control. It does not claim every behavior of a general-purpose OS TCP stack. Stateless replay instead preserves recorded frame bytes; it makes no live connection-state claim.</p>
  <h2 id="qualification">Inspect the evidence, not a badge</h2><p>The v{release.version} release has public software-lab qualification records for Windows and Linux x64. Those records separate application, raw packet, and stateless replay cases, including secure application cases and fault handling. Handshake-only evidence establishes a fresh TLS connection, not an application response match.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Hardware qualification is a separate step.</strong>Software-lab evidence does not establish performance or behavior on your physical NIC, industrial device, firewall, or network topology. Native ARM64 execution and human browser testing are not implied by a cross-built artifact.</div><p><a href={source(release.qualificationPath)}>Open the qualification record ↗</a> · <a href={sitePath("/reference/reliability/")}>Read implementation boundaries →</a></p>
</Guide>
